{"title":"2017 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8) Problems/Problem 17","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems/Problem_17&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>On coach Wooden’s basketball team:\n</p>\n<ul style=\"list-style-type: disc;\">\n<li>Meghan is the tallest player,</li>\n<li>Meghan’s height is 188 cm, and</li>\n<li>Avery is the shortest player.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>When used with the information above, which of the following single statements is enough to determine Avery’s height?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/e/b6ead23ade3d9ee1aa74316382a105b34c42a323.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A)}\\ \\text{The median of the players&#39; heights is 170 cm} \\qquad\\textbf{(B)}\\ \\text{The mode of the players&#39; heights is 160 cm} \\qquad\\textbf{(C)}\\ \\text{The mean of the players&#39; heights is 165 cm} \\qquad\\textbf{(D)}\\ \\text{The range of the players&#39; heights is 33 cm} \\qquad\\textbf{(E)}\\ \\text{There are 10 players on the team}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"1801\" height=\"18\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems/Problem_17&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We can look at each answer choice until we find one that makes sense.\n</p><p>For answer choices A, B, C, and E, we don't know enough information because we don't know the heights for the other players or the sum of the heights. However, the range can be used because it is always equal to the greatest height minus the lowest height, and we know Meghan's height and that Meghan is the tallest, as well as the fact that Avery is the shortest.\n</p><p>Thus, the answer must be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/e/a/5ea40074cc7fb18ab8891c90ed76758fbb3da0fe.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf {(D) } \\text{The range of the players&#39; heights is 33 cm}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"353\" height=\"30\" />.\n</p><p>~anabel.disher\n</p>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)\" title=\"2017 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8)\">2017 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8)</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"2017 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8) Problems (page does not exist)\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Answer_Key&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"2017 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8) Answer Key (page does not exist)\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://cemc.math.uwaterloo.ca/contests/past_contests.html\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems/Problem_16\" title=\"2017 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8) Problems/Problem 16\">Problem 16</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems/Problem_18\" title=\"2017 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8) Problems/Problem 18\">Problem 18</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems/Pro